{{To get started with a new Django project, the first step is to ensure you have Python installed on your system, as Django is a Python-based web framework. You can verify your Python installation by running `python --version` in your terminal or command prompt. Once Python is confirmed, you'll need to install Django. It's a good practice to use a virtual environment for your project to manage dependencies effectively. You can create a virtual environment by executing `python -m venv myenv`, where `myenv` is your desired environment name. Activate the virtual environment with `source myenv/bin/activate` on macOS and Linux or `myenv\Scripts\activate` on Windows. Next, install Django by running `pip install django`, which will download the latest version of Django from the Python Package Index (PyPI).}}

{{After installing Django, you can create a new project by using the Django administrative command-line utility. Run `django-admin startproject myproject`, replacing `myproject` with your desired project name. This command generates a new directory with the project's name, containing the necessary files and folders that form the backbone of your Django application. Inside this directory, you will find a `manage.py` file, which is a command-line tool that allows you to interact with your project, and a subdirectory with the same name as your project, containing essential files such as `settings.py`, `urls.py`, and `wsgi.py`. These files are crucial for configuring settings, defining URL patterns, and setting up WSGI applications for deployment, respectively.}}

{{With your Django project set up, you can now proceed to run the development server to test your installation. Navigate to your project directory where the `manage.py` file is located and run the command `python manage.py runserver`. By default, Django will start a lightweight development server on `http://127.0.0.1:8000/`. Open this URL in your web browser, and you should see the Django welcome page, confirming that your setup is successful. From here, you can begin building your application's features by creating new Django apps within the project using `python manage.py startapp appname`, setting up models, views, templates, and configuring URLs as needed. Remember to regularly test your application and consult the official Django documentation for more advanced configurations and best practices.}}

